---------GameMaker Tutorial---------

    When opening the software, and creating a new empty file, you catch yourself with the Toolbar (Located above, where you can run the game), Inspector (Located left, where you can see and change the properties of the asset you've selected), Output Window (Located below, where you can see any errors or debug messages), Asset Browser (Located right, where you create and organise your game's assets/objects like images/sprites, code and rooms/game levels), and finally, the Workspace (Located in the middle)
    When creating a sprite, you can choose the canvas size, create the visual using brushs and even create a brush using Ctrl + C. For animating, you can choose the FPS (Frames per Second) value and add new frames as much as you want.
    When creating an object, you can asset a name, sprite and events, inside an event you can script what will happen envolving the object.
    When creating a room, you have instances and background. The background is where your character will be displayed above it, without collisions, like a floor. The Instances is where you can put your objects, it can eigther be a character or furnitures, for example.

Coding
    Variable is a storage location in the computer's memory paired with a name, that contains data. When naming a variable, you can put letters (which are case sensitive), numbers and underscores, remember that you hav to make clear and descriptive names, with readability and consistency. To set a variable:

player_name = "link";
player_health = 3;
player_weapon = "sword";

    To use (reference) Data:

show_degub_message(player_name);
show_degub_message(player_health);
show_degub_message(player_weapon);

    Data Type is an attribute of the data which tells a comuter how to treat that data, it defines the operations to treat that data. The common data types are: strings, real numbers, booleans and arrays. The less common data types are Enums and Undefined. The rare data types are Nan, Infinity, Hexadecimals and Pointers.
    1. String is a text, defined by quotation marks ("").
    2. Real Numbers are defined by any number (positive, negative, etc).
    3. A Boolean is  avalue that can be either true or false
    4. Arrays are a type of data that holds other types of datas, it is a reference to the data. They're defined by brakets [], dividing each data with ", ".

    Variable Scope is the place where you can validly reference variable by name, it allows variables to have the same name. There are three scope categories: global, instance and local.
    1. The Global Scope can be accessed by everything, have a special declaration and remain in memory until the game ends. (global.example)
    2. The Instance Scope are created within an instance of an object, unique to that instance.
    3. The Local Scope are created for a specific event or script, have a special declaration (var example) discarded when the event has finished.

    Enum is a custom data type, a named integer, referenced anywhere, you get to name a number. They're useful for readable, searchable and maintainable code, for example: player_state = states.idle instead of player_state = 0 or 1. A Enum can be created everywhere, must be unique and cannot be changed after the game is compiled. When creating an Enum:

enum player_states {
    idle,  // automatically means 0
    walk,  // automatically means 1
    run,   // automatically means 2
    attack // automatically means 3
}

enum inventory_slots {
    name = 0   // you're defining it equals to 0
    amount = 1 // you're defining it equals to 1
}

    After creating a Enum, you can use it by:
// same as saying state = 0;
state = player_states.idle;

// same as saying item_name = inventory_array[0];
item_name = inventory_array[inventory_slots.name];

    Macro is a single word that stands for something else, such as a single value or a complex expression, they are a text replacement (copy and paste), constants (cannot be changed). They're efficient because of readable, searchable and maintainable code, for example: item = NONE; or, for example: if (COIN_FLIP) {}. A Macro can be created anywhere, must be unique and cannot be changed after the game is compiled. To create a Macro:

// #macro name replacement_text
#macro NONE -1
#macro SETUP_EVENT 0
#macro PAUSE_EVENT 10
#macro MY_PI 3.14
#macro BLUE $FF0000
#macro FLIP_COIN choose(true, false)

    Macro examples:

// using a macro instead of hex code
image_blen = BLUE;

// using a macro to shorten things
if (FLIP_COIN) {
    // do a thing
}

    The difference about Macros and Enums is that Enums are a data type and named integers, while Macros are text replacement and can be used for complex expressions.

