    Booleans are a thing which can have one of two values (true or false), for example: the statement "two plus two equal to four" can be true or false. The Boolean logic is an evaluation of truth or falsity of a combination of one or more true or false statements, for example: If it is raining or the sprinkler is on, the grass will be wet. The Boolean Operators are And (&&), Or (||), Xor (^^) and Not (!).

    For example:
A is true
B is false

    Not:
A (true)
!A (false)

B (false)
!B (true)

    And:
A (true), B (true), A && B (true)
A (true), B (false), A && B (false)
A (false), B (true), A && B (false)
A (false), B (false), A && B (false)

    Or:
A (true), B (true), A || B (true)
A (true), B (false), A || B (true)
A (false), B (true), A || B (true)
A (false), B (false), A || B (false)

    Xor:
A (true), B (true), A ^^ B (false)
A (true), B (false), A ^^ B (true)
A (false), B (true), A ^^ B (true)
A (false), B (false), A ^^ B (false)

